      prompt:
`You are an Industrial Engineer working for Big Box Warehouses (BBW), a regional membership-only wholesale retail store chain. BBW is in the process of modernizing its stores, including replacing the cashier checkout stations and introducing self-checkout stations. A proof-of-concept project was already conducted at one of the stores to test the new stations, and now you have been assigned to the project implementing them in the remaining stores.
One of your main assignments is to determine the checkout stations configuration for each store. Each store can have a different configuration depending on its checkout traffic. One of the main complaints from BBW's customers is the long checkout lines, so one of the objectives of your project, dictated by the company, is that the average time in queue should always be less than 10 minutes.
The manager of the project decided to start the project at store #115, and assigned you the following initial tasks:
• Analyze the data provided in the store_115_data.xlsx and CAU_stations_PoC.xlsx files to determine the probability distributions required to simulate this system. Include the mean for the selected distributions.
• Create conceptual models for potential configurations of the checkout system. Use the additional information provided in the proof_of_concept_summary.pdf and store_profile_115.pdf.
• Use discrete event simulation to determine the checkout stations configuration that minimize the number of cashiers required to satisfy checkout demand for store #115 while keeping average waiting time under 10 minutes. Perform the study as of May 29, 2026. Include number of replications and replication length used on the study.
• Include a flowchart describing the simulation logic implemented.
• Calculate how many employee hours are required in the checkout area for each shift. Use proof_of_concept_summary.pdf and store_profile_115.pdf files for additional information required for this analysis.
• Write a report of 10 pages or less, detailing your methodology and results. Results representing discrete quantities must be reported in integer values; use two decimal points for results representing non-discrete quantities. Return the report in a file called "checkout_configuration_report_115.pdf".`,
